// GTlsConnection and GTlsClientConnection (gtlsconnection.h,
// gtlsclientconnection.h): TLS over a GIOStream, as libsoup makes and
// uses it, on std.tls (TLS 1.3 and 1.2) and the backend's databases and
// certificates (tls.mlx).
//
// A connection is a GIOStream over its base stream ("base-io-stream",
// a GSocketConnection mostly): its input and output streams decrypt and
// encrypt, the handshake running first when it has not been asked for.
// std.tls does no I/O of its own: the bytes it has to send are written
// to the base output stream and what the base input stream gives is fed
// to it, blocking for the synchronous calls, without blocking for the
// asynchronous handshake and the pollable streams (they wait on the base
// streams' sources), so the handshake and reads run in the caller's main
// context, as GIO's GnuTLS backend runs them. A chain that does not
// verify against the connection's database (the system's by default) for
// the server identity emits "accept-certificate"; refused, the handshake
// fails with G_TLS_ERROR_BAD_CERTIFICATE, "Unacceptable TLS certificate".
// The peer's certificate, its errors, the protocol version, the suite's
// name (IANA's, as GnuTLS gives them) and the protocol ALPN agreed on
// are the connection's properties once the handshake is done, notified
// as they change.
//
// Not here: client certificates (an empty one is sent when a server asks),
// session resumption, rehandshakes (TLS 1.3 has none; TLS 1.2's are
// refused), channel bindings, servers.
const m = @import("./memory.mlx")
const threads = @import("./threads.mlx")
const errors = @import("./errors.mlx")
const types = @import("./types.mlx")
const signals = @import("./signals.mlx")
const ioerrors = @import("./ioerrors.mlx")
const tls = @import("std.tls")
const x509 = @import("std.x509")

